Debug.Assert(_size - _defaultCapacity == 0);
</code>

Explanation: In the ArrayList.cs file, the default capacity of the ArrayList is 4 and the variable "capacity" is set to the array list size (at the end of every method, including constructor). The initial size of the array is also the default capacity. So the assertion checks that the difference between the current size and the default capacity should be zero. This is because for every operation like Add, Remove etc., if the list's size exceeds the capacity, the capacity will be doubled to accommodate it. Hence if the array's size (not initial capacity) is still equal to the default at the beginning, the size will eventually exceed it.

<code>
Debug.Assert(_size - _defaultCapacity == 0);
Debug.Assert(_syncRoot == obj);
</code>

These two assertion checks ensure that the _syncRoot variable will be non-null if and only if the passed in object is the same instance as the ArrayList itself. This is how the synchronization for the ArrayList class is done. 

<code>
Debug.Assert(index + 1 < _size);
</code>

This is true when the index of the first occurrence of the element to be removed is valid, i.e., it is less than the size of the list but not equal to it. This ensures that the element to be removed is a valid one and doesn't exceed the bounds of the list.--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(Capacity) == OLD(Count));
Debug.Assert(RET >= OLD(Count));
Debug.Assert(RET >= Capacity);
</code>

Also note that C# compiler does not guarantee the exact sequence in which properties and fields are initialized so you might need more conditions to assert this property in code itself.

<code>
Debug.Assert(RET != null && RET.Count == OLD(Count) && (RET.Count <= RET.Capacity));
</code>

Also note that the `NonSerialized` attribute on `_syncRoot` might be part of the scenario leading us to skip this line. Some other details could be ignored as well like the constructor definitions which might take some more conditions or even more details.

<code>
Debug.Assert(RET == null || (RET.Count == OLD(Count) && (RET.Count <= RET.Capacity) && RET.Count >= 0));
</code>

In the case where `Add` method is added, you might need more conditions to assert the exact size increase.

<code>
Debug.Assert(RET != null && RET.Count == OLD(Count) + 1 && RET.Count <= RET.Capacity && OLD(Count) >= 0);
</code>

Also, in the case when the collection type may contain duplicates, it should include `RET == OLD(RET).Count`.

<code>
Debug.Assert(RET.Count == OLD(RET).Count);
</code>
  
